{-
   The map literal, compiled by both resolve stages - Implementation-Map.md §7.

   `test/resolve/MapLiteral.yana` is the whole feature and is the shipping stage's alone. This one is
   here because `test/lib` is compiled against the real library by both stages, which is what a map
   literal needs; it was written here when `test/resolve` still compiled the rewrite against a
   stand-in library with no `Map` in it.

   The compiler builds no map. `[k: v]` is `known.def`'s `mapLiteral` role - `newMap(n)`, the one
   construction site where the entry count is a compile-time constant - and one
   `IndexInsert.insertAt` per pair, left to right, which is the same member `m[k] = v` reaches. No
   field of `Map` is read by either stage.

   Four claims, each of which fails on its own:

    - **A literal with an expected type.** `newMap` names neither `k` nor `v` in its arguments, so
      the result position is the only thing that decides them.
    - **`[:]` is a usable map** rather than a value that only type-checks; inserting into one and
      reading it back is the whole of that difference.
    - **`m[k] = v` over an absent key inserts.** `getMut` cannot express that - there is nothing to
      borrow for a key the map has not got - which is why `IndexInsert` exists, and a literal is that
      member applied `n` times.
    - **Two equal keys are one entry and the later wins**, which follows from the line above rather
      than being a rule of its own.

   `Int` keys throughout, deliberately: a `Map(String, v)` does not yet *run* under the rewrite (the
   hash reaches the string's own storage), and this fixture is about the literal.
-}

import Test

@test fn literalWithAType():
    let m = [1: 10, 2: 20] :: Map(Int, Int)
    checkEqual(m[1], 10)
    checkEqual(m[2], 20)
    checkEqual(truncate(length(m)) :: Int, 2)

@test fn emptyLiteralIsAMap():
    let &m = [:] :: Map(Int, Int)
    checkEqual(truncate(length(m)) :: Int, 0)

    m[7] = 3
    checkEqual(m[7], 3)
    checkEqual(truncate(length(m)) :: Int, 1)

@test fn assignmentInsertsAndReplaces():
    let &m = [1: 10] :: Map(Int, Int)
    m[2] = 20
    m[1] = 40

    checkEqual(m[1], 40)
    checkEqual(m[2], 20)
    checkEqual(truncate(length(m)) :: Int, 2)

@test fn theLaterOfTwoEqualKeysWins():
    let m = [5: 1, 5: 2] :: Map(Int, Int)
    checkEqual(m[5], 2)
    checkEqual(truncate(length(m)) :: Int, 1)

@test fn theBracketTypeSpelling():
    let m = [1: 2] :: [Int: Int]
    checkEqual(m[1], 2)
